The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (test_opt(sb, DISCARD)) {
    flush_work(&sbi->s_discard_work);
    WARN_ON_ONCE(!list_empty(&sbi->s_discard_list));
}
Reason:
The conditional guarded the flush_work and warning only when the DISCARD mount option was set. If the filesystem was remounted with nodiscard after discard operations had been queued, the flag is cleared, so the pending discard work is never drained during unmount. This leaves ext4_free_data structures on the discard list, potentially causing memory corruption or use-after-free.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
flush_work(&sbi->s_discard_work);
WARN_ON_ONCE(!list_empty(&sbi->s_discard_list));
Reason:
By removing the conditional, the discard work is always drained when the filesystem is released, regardless of the current DISCARD mount option. This ensures that any pending discard work from earlier operations is completed before unmount, preventing resource leaks and use-after-free.

